ROWS = [
dict(id="T228", d="BM", t="Exact persistence exponents for diffusion from Gaussian white noise",
 s="Let phi_d(x,t) solve the heat equation on R^d from centered Gaussian spatial white noise. Determine theta(d) in P(phi_d(0,t)>0 for every 1<=t<=T)=T^(-theta(d)+o(1)) for integers d>=1 other than d=2. Equivalently, determine the persistence exponent of the centered stationary unit-variance Gaussian process with covariance sech((s-t)/2)^(d/2).",
 c="Time starts at 1 because white noise has no pointwise initial value. Normalizing the field and changing to logarithmic time gives the stated stationary covariance. The exponent's existence is known; its exact value is the issue. Dimension two is solved and is explicitly excluded. T226 concerns integrated fractional Brownian motion, whose stationary transform has a different covariance; it is not a reformulation of this heat-equation problem.",
 src="M. Poplavskyi and G. Schehr, Exact persistence exponent for the 2d-diffusion equation and related Kac polynomials, arXiv:1806.11275 (2018), Introduction; A. Dembo and S. Mukherjee, No zero-crossings for random polynomials and the heat equation, arXiv:1208.2382v4 (2015)",
 loc="Poplavskyi-Schehr, PDF p.1: 'Although several very accurate approximation schemes exist to compute θ(d)' they explain the lack of an exact value, and then solve d=2. The same page gives c(T)=[sech(T/2)]^(d/2). Dembo-Mukherjee's abstract gives the rigorous heat-equation exponent b_(d/2-1).",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.11275",
 why="Persistence measures whether the entire time history of a fluctuating field avoids a sign change. Even the linear diffusion equation produces a non-Markov Gaussian process at one spatial point, so this is a particularly simple model with a difficult and nontrivial first-passage exponent.",
 st="Dembo and Mukherjee establish existence and identify theta(d) with the Gaussian persistence constant b_(d/2-1). Poplavskyi and Schehr compute theta(2)=3/16 by a special integrable correspondence. The inspected 2024 universality result for random polynomials retains the other b_alpha as persistence constants. Dornic and Conte's arXiv:2603.28632 studies the integrable sech kernel and the associated 3/16 case; it does not compute theta(d) for the remaining diffusion dimensions. Open.",
 n="Fame: a central persistence problem of nonequilibrium statistical physics since the 1990s, treated in major persistence surveys and connected rigorously to random-polynomial zero probabilities."),

dict(id="T229", d="IPS", t="Critical exponents of directed percolation in one spatial dimension",
 s="On the oriented lattice with edges (x,t) to (x-1,t+1) and (x+1,t+1), independently open every edge with probability p. Let p_c be the threshold for an infinite oriented open path. Prove existence and determine the value of the critical survival exponent delta defined by P_(p_c)((0,0) has an open path to time t)=t^(-delta+o(1)). This is the basic survival-exponent problem for the (1+1)-dimensional directed-percolation universality class.",
 c="The preferred direction is time; this is not isotropic planar percolation and its exactly known exponents do not apply. This row targets ordinary short-range directed percolation, with a single absorbing state. Conserved, parity-conserving, quantum, long-range and quenched-disorder models can have different critical behavior. T001 and T087 concern undirected percolation; T005 concerns the distinct KPZ universality class.",
 src="H. Hinrichsen, Non-equilibrium critical phenomena and phase transitions into absorbing states, arXiv:cond-mat/0001070 (2000), Sections 3.1 and 3.4",
 loc="Section 3.1, PDF p.35: 'the numerical values of the critical point and the exponents of DP are not yet known analytically'. Section 3.4 defines the critical survival law P(t) and its exponent delta, with delta=beta'/nu_parallel.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0001070",
 why="Directed percolation is the standard critical model for epidemics, extinction and phase transitions into an absorbing state. Its one-dimensional critical behavior is numerically very well characterized but lacks the exact or rigorous exponent theory available for many equilibrium planar models.",
 st="The inspected recent papers on ordinary directed percolation use numerical or scaling methods; rigorous contact-process papers for special networks address different geometries or extinction scales. The 2026 exact constants-of-motion paper and the quantum or hybrid contact-process papers do not determine the ordinary critical survival exponent. Results for parity-conserving directed percolation or for models with long jumps likewise do not supply it. Open.",
 n="Fame: the directed-percolation universality class is the standard absorbing-state critical phenomenon and the subject of Hinrichsen's extensive review; determining its low-dimensional critical exponents is a longstanding central problem."),

dict(id="T230", d="STAT", t="The low-rate reliability function of the binary symmetric channel",
 s="For the memoryless binary symmetric channel with crossover probability 0<p<1/2, determine its optimal block-coding reliability function E(R,p)=limsup_(n to infinity) -(1/n) log P_e^*(n,R,p), including the unresolved interval of small positive rates. Here P_e^* is the smallest average decoding-error probability over binary block codes with at least ceil(exp(nR)) equiprobable messages, without feedback; logarithms and rates are in nats, and 0<R<log(2)-h(p).",
 c="The channel capacity is known; the question is the sharp exponential decay of the best attainable error probability at a fixed rate below capacity. The positive-rate limit R down to zero must not be confused with a fixed two-message problem. Error exponents for a specified random-code ensemble, decoder or code family need not equal the optimum over all codes. Feedback and identification coding define different reliability problems. T117 concerns capacity of a deletion channel, not the reliability of a memoryless substitution channel.",
 src="A. Somekh-Baruch, An Upper Bound on the Reliability Function of the DMC, arXiv:2209.00968 (2022), Introduction; M. V. Burnashev, Code Spectrum and Reliability Function: Binary Symmetric Channel, arXiv:cs/0612032 (2006), Section I",
 loc="Somekh-Baruch, PDF p.1: deriving the reliability formula 'for a certain range of low rates remains an open problem'. Burnashev, PDF pp.1-2, defines E(R,p) for the BSC and lists the known zero-rate and high-rate portions. His coding rate is in bits; the row converts it to nats.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.00968",
 why="The binary symmetric channel is the fundamental model of independent transmission errors. Capacity describes which error probabilities can vanish; the reliability function asks how quickly optimal coding can make them vanish. The low-rate gap is one of the oldest quantitative gaps between coding achievability and converse bounds.",
 st="The exact function is known in a high-rate range, and the limit as the positive rate decreases to zero is known. Somekh-Baruch improves converse bounds while explicitly retaining the low-rate problem. The inspected 2022-2026 results on decoder ties, typical random-coding exponents, moderate deviations, low-rate Reed-Muller decoding and deterministic identification do not identify the unrestricted fixed-positive-rate optimum. A 2022 zero-rate result with noiseless feedback concerns a different operational model. Open.",
 n="Fame: a classical Shannon-Gallager-Berlekamp reliability problem studied since the 1960s, with decades of coding-theory bounds and continuing work even for this simplest noisy channel.")
]
